# CHEESE indexed database catalog.
#
# One line per database available for on-prem download:
#
#     <folder>|<bytes>|<objects>
#
# `folder` is the folder name in the delivery bucket and on disk. `bytes` and
# `objects` are informational — `cheese configure-dbs` shows the size so you can
# check free disk space before starting, and `cheese download-dbs` uses the total
# for its space preflight. A blank size means "not measured"; the download still
# works, the estimate is just incomplete.
#
# Why a shipped file instead of listing the bucket: the read-only role your access
# key assumes can read the database folders but deliberately cannot list the
# bucket root, which also holds unrelated internal data. So the set of databases
# is published here. `cheese configure-dbs` checks every entry against the bucket,
# so a stale line shows up as "NOT AVAILABLE" rather than a silent gap.
#
# ⚠️ THIS LIST MUST BE A SUBSET OF WHAT THE HOSTED SERVICE SERVES.
# We never offer on-prem a database we do not serve online — fewer is fine, more
# is not. The authoritative list is:
#
#     curl -s https://cheese.deepmedchem.com/api/available_dbs
#
# which returns CANONICAL names; map them to the folders below with the
# DB_CANONICAL_NAME table in scripts/_engine-config. A folder with no canonical
# name cannot be offered at all — there is nothing for a customer to key it by.
# scripts/assert-catalog-subset.sh checks this and runs in CI.
#
# Keep in sync with: the cheese-onprem-db-read IAM policy (which enumerates the
# same prefixes) and the DB_CANONICAL_NAME table in scripts/_engine-config.

